</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="exposure">
<h2>Exposure calculations</h2>
<p>NOAA did not publish exactly how it calculated exposure in its Coastal County Snapshots tool. This tool calculates exposure by intersecting the floodplain and sea level rise extents with <a href="https://www.census.gov/newsroom/blogs/random-samplings/2011/07/what-are-census-blocks.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Census block level</a> data, which is the smallest geography available. <a href="https://lehd.ces.census.gov/data/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Census LODES</a> provides jobs at the per-block level. <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/data.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Census ACS</a> provides population and poverty at the block group level, and is distributed to blocks based on 2020 Census distribution. This allows for splitting of exposure based on partial overlap; for example, if a block is 30% inside of a floodplain, 30% of people and jobs are counted.</p>
<p>Facilities are a simple count of plotted points inside the mapped area. Flood insurance claims are not mapped, and are totalled per county.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="connected-low-lying">
<h2>Connected and low-lying sea level rise exposure</h2>

<p>NOAA's Coastal County Snapshots seemed to only tabulate direct exposure to sea level rise in some counties, when the OPC guidance recommends considering all low-lying areas which are also exposed to risk, for example from groundwater intrusion (p. 48) or coastal storms (p. 52). This tool includes exposure to these areas indicated differently (dashed bars). This data is built on areas identified by NOAA in the <a href="https://coast.noaa.gov/data/digitalcoast/pdf/slr-inundation-methods.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sea Level Rise Viewer method description</a>; as noted however, this doesn't account for infrastructure like storm drains and ditches, so the impact is less certain but nonetheless still a risk. In some places, the difference is small. In others, however, the difference is stark: for example, compare San Mateo's <a href="{{ '/county-profiles/county/san-mateo/sea-level-rise/' | url }}#s-people-at-risk" target="_blank">CASCA County Profile</a> to <a href="https://coast.noaa.gov/snapshots/#&state=eyJmaXBzIjoiMDYwODEiLCJzbmFwc2hvdElEIjoiZnV0dXJlRmxvb2QiLCJzZWN0aW9uSUQiOiJmdXR1cmVEZW1vZ3JhcGhpY3MiLCJzbHJWYWx1ZSI6IjIifQ==" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA's Coastal County Snapshot</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="land-cover">
<h2>Land cover</h2>
<p>The natural-features sections use NOAA's 1996 and 2021 <a href="https://coast.noaa.gov/digitalcoast/data/ccapregional.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">C-CAP regional land cover data</a> at 30 meters, which uses the latest update (NOAA's tool was using 2016 C-CAP data). Wetland is the palustrine and estuarine wetland and aquatic-bed classes; upland is grassland, forest, scrub and shrub; other is developed, cultivated, pasture, bare and shore land. Open water is not counted as land. "Development added" is the difference between undeveloped land in 1996 and developed land in 2021. Inside and outside the floodplain, and inside each sea level rise extent, are pixel counts against the same hazard polygons as every other section.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="map-links">
<h2>View on map links</h2>
<p>The original NOAA tool generated and cached inset maps for each topic. This feature is removed in favor of a link to the <a href="{{ '/map/' | url }}">CASCA map tool</a> with the relevant layers enabled; the table below lists the details. Because the CASCA map data is loaded live instead of being cached on the server, adding another layer of caching and processing while also supporting snapshotting for this tool seemed time and storage intensive. Map links are omitted for some sections for which data is available but is not built into the map tool, like the FEMA NFIP community layer. Dated snapshots explicitly note the map data is current, implying there may be some drift.</p>
<p>Note that the map <strong>does not exactly reproduce the County Profile topic</strong> because the data behind County Profiles do not always have a 1:1 mapping layer, and in some cases the map layer presents a different source entirely. Consider the map a separate way to assess the same hazard instead of an extension of the County Profiles tool.</p>
